<div class="sect1">
<div class="titlepage"><div><div><h2 class="title" style="clear: both">
<a name="zend.feed.consuming-atom-single-entry"></a>33.6. Consuming a Single Atom Entry</h2></div></div></div>
<p>
        Single Atom <span class="command"><strong>&lt;entry&gt;</strong></span> elements are also valid by themselves. Usually
        the <acronym class="acronym">URL</acronym> for an entry is the feed's <acronym class="acronym">URL</acronym> followed by
        <span class="command"><strong>/&lt;entryId&gt;</strong></span>, such as
        <code class="filename">http://atom.example.com/feed/1</code>, using the example
        <acronym class="acronym">URL</acronym> we used above.
    </p>
<p>
        If you read a single entry, you will still have a <code class="classname">Zend_Feed_Atom</code>
        object, but it will automatically create an "anonymous" feed to contain the entry.
    </p>
<div class="example">
<a name="zend.feed.consuming-atom-single-entry.example.atom"></a><p class="title"><b>Example 33.3. Reading a Single-Entry Atom Feed</b></p>
<div class="example-contents"><pre class="programlisting">
$feed = new Zend_Feed_Atom('http://atom.example.com/feed/1');
echo 'The feed has: ' . $feed-&gt;count() . ' entry.';

$entry = $feed-&gt;current();
</pre></div>
</div>
<br class="example-break"><p>
        Alternatively, you could instantiate the entry object directly if you know you are accessing
        an <span class="command"><strong>&lt;entry&gt;</strong></span>-only document:
    </p>
<div class="example">
<a name="zend.feed.consuming-atom-single-entry.example.entryatom"></a><p class="title"><b>Example 33.4. Using the Entry Object Directly for a Single-Entry Atom Feed</b></p>
<div class="example-contents"><pre class="programlisting">
$entry = new Zend_Feed_Entry_Atom('http://atom.example.com/feed/1');
echo $entry-&gt;title();
</pre></div>
</div>
<br class="example-break">
</div>
